    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §4, Chap 522 of 2000",
    "lawSection": "State Finance",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S8272\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend chapter 522 of the laws of 2000, amending the state finance law\nand the general business law relating to establishing the underground\nfacilities safety training account, in relation to extending provisions\nof law relating to the underground facilities safety training account\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nTo extend provisions of law relating\nto the underground facilities safety training account from October 1,\n2005 until October 1, 2015.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nSection 1 amends section 4 of Chapter\n522 of the laws of 2000, as amended by Chapter 707 of the laws of\n2005, establishing the underground safety training account, to extend\nthe program until October 1, 2015\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nThe one-call notification system established by the\nState has made significant progress in reducing the number of\naccidents involving underground utility facilities by providing\ncontractors and others who have occasion to do underground excavation\nwith a single place to which they can go to ascertain the location of\nall underground facilities in the area in which they plan, to dig.\nHowever, the great majority of accidents which do occur with such\nfacilities are still caused by excavations by those who are ignorant\nof, or choose not to avail themselves of, the one-call system. The\navailability of funding through this safety account has been\nsuccessful in providing training and education to operators and\nexcavators on the requirements and procedures for the protection of\nunderground facilities.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nChapter 522 of 2000, Chapter 707 or 2005.\nThe current law expires on October 1, 2005\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nThis bill will reduce New York energy costs while\nhaving a very minimal impact on the state's general revenues.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n\n",
